#---
# purpose: "an Enum.with_index/2 user whose lambda declares a @spec is not made to answer for a neighbour's element type"
#---
#
# fz-kdt.183's live reproducer, and the shortest statement of the defect: the
# two `dbg` lines below share no value, no lambda and no type, and at HEAD the
# first one failed to compile BECAUSE OF THE SECOND.
#
# The chain is `Enum.with_index/2` -> `Enum.to_list/1` -> `Enum.reverse/1` ->
# `Enum.reduce/3` -> `Enumerable.reduce/3` -> `Enumerable.List.reduce/3` ->
# `List.reduce/3` -> `List.reduce_step/3`. Every link forwards its list
# unchanged, and `List.reduce_step/3`'s own dispatch asks only about the
# accumulator tag, so the recursive collapse erased the element and ONE
# activation stood for both users. The one return it published was the join,
# `[[binary] | [int]]`, and `M.strings/1` was handed a list of those.
#
# Deleting the second line made the first compile. That is the whole finding:
# an activation's published return may depend only on what its key names, and
# the key did not name the element the callee's key is ground in.
#
# The runtime helper's declared list and mapper domains make its empty and cons
# clauses exhaustive. Both users therefore compile without diagnostics as well
# as retaining their independent element types.
defmodule M do
  @spec strings([binary]) :: integer
  def strings(_xs), do: 1
end

def main() do
  dbg(Enum.with_index([["a"], ["b"]], fn (x, _index) -> M.strings(x) end))
  dbg(Enum.with_index([[1], [2]], fn (_x, index) -> index end))
end
